<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/heat-treatment">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Heat treatment is <strong>microstructure by itinerary</strong>: identical chemistry, a
    different temperature–time path, a different material. A eutectoid steel can be soft
    enough to machine or hard enough to cut glass depending on nothing but how fast it was
    cooled from 800 °C. The blacksmith's hiss of a blade entering the quench tank was this
    lesson in empirical form; the phase-diagram lesson supplied the map, and this lesson adds
    the physics the map leaves out — <strong>time</strong>. Two forces set every treatment:
    thermodynamics says which state the atoms <em>want</em>, and diffusion sets how fast they
    can <em>get there</em>. Heat treatment is the deliberate engineering of the race between
    them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="quench, temper, anneal, case-harden" widget="equationStep" config={heatCfg} />

  <h2>Diffusion I: the mechanism and Fick's laws</h2>
  <p>
    Atoms in a crystal move by <strong>hopping</strong>: an atom swaps places with a vacancy
    (substitutional diffusion — slow, needs vacancies) or squeezes between lattice sites
    (interstitial diffusion — the carbon-in-iron case, much faster because carbon is tiny).
    Either way motion is a biased random walk down the concentration gradient, which Fick
    wrote as a law: <strong>flux J = −D·(dC/dx)</strong> — atoms flow from where they are
    concentrated to where they are not, at a rate proportional to the gradient, scaled by the
    <strong>diffusivity</strong> D. The unsteady version, Fick's second law, describes the
    gradient itself smoothing over time, and its solutions carry the field's most useful
    rule of thumb: the characteristic penetration depth grows as <strong>√(Dt)</strong>.
    Quadruple the time and the case deepens only twofold; the arithmetic of every
    carburising schedule follows from that square root.
  </p>
  <p>
    D itself obeys an Arrhenius law, D = D₀·exp(−Q/RT), because every hop must clear an
    energy barrier. Worked example, carbon in FCC iron (austenite): D₀ ≈ 2.3 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s
    with activation energy Q ≈ 148 kJ/mol. At 900 °C (1173 K) the exponential gives
    D ≈ 5.9 × 10⁻¹² m²/s; at 1000 °C (1273 K), D ≈ 1.9 × 10⁻¹¹ m²/s — a hundred-degree
    furnace change buys a <strong>3× speed-up</strong>. This is the tyranny and the gift of
    the exponential: diffusion is pathologically temperature-sensitive, so kilns compete
    over tens of degrees, and the same sensitivity is why a quench (cool through hundreds
    of degrees in seconds) can effectively stop atomic motion mid-flight.
  </p>

  <h2>Diffusion II: time–temperature and the TTT map</h2>
  <p>
    The equilibrium phase diagram knows nothing of time; the fix is the
    <strong>TTT (time–temperature–transformation) diagram</strong>, drawn for one alloy: for
    each temperature below the austenite field, a curve marks when transformation
    <em>begins</em> and when it <em>finishes</em>. The curve is C-shaped: at high temperature
    transformation is slow because the driving force is small; at low temperature it is slow
    because diffusion is; the fastest transformation sits in the nose of the C (~550 °C for
    eutectoid steel). Every treatment is a path drawn on this map. <strong>Anneal</strong>:
    cool slowly down the right side — diffusion keeps pace, coarse <strong>pearlite</strong>
    forms, maximum softness. <strong>Normalise</strong>: cool faster in air — finer
    pearlite, uniform grain, a machinable compromise. <strong>Quench</strong>: cool faster
    than the nose can catch — you miss every equilibrium product and land in a regime the
    equilibrium diagram does not contain: <strong>martensite</strong>, carbon trapped in a
    distorted BCT lattice (the FCC-to-BCC transition happens without diffusion, and the
    trapped interstitials strain it), extremely hard (about 65 HRC) and dangerously brittle.
    <strong>Temper</strong> finishes the job: re-warming to 150–650 °C lets carbon escape
    into fine carbides and stresses relax — hardness falls a little (to, say, 55–60 HRC),
    toughness rises enormously. The classic schedule — quench, then temper — is a
    thermodynamic cheat followed by a negotiated settlement. A companion diagram, the
    <strong>CCT</strong> (continuous-cooling) version, adapts TTT to real furnace ramps;
    and the <strong>Jominy end-quench test</strong> measures a grade's
    <em>hardenability</em> — how deep below the surface a quench can still outrun the nose,
    which is alloying's real service: boron or chromium shift the C-curve right, buying the
    quench more time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Follow one part through the whole map. Take a 0.8 % carbon (eutectoid) steel shaft,
    25 mm across. Austenitise at 800 °C for half an hour — long enough that the √(Dt) rule
    has carried carbon homogeneously through the section. Quench in agitated water: the
    surface plunges past the nose in seconds, misses every equilibrium product, and
    transforms below its martensite-start into full martensite at roughly 65 HRC — though
    it does so while carrying maximum thermal stress, which is why high-carbon grades are
    the ones that quench-crack. Temper at 400 °C for an hour: hardness settles near 45 HRC
    and the part is spring-tough. The identical steel, furnace-cooled instead, arrives at
    coarse pearlite around 20 HRC. Same alloy, a 45-point hardness spread, chosen entirely
    by itinerary — and note what the example does <em>not</em> involve: no composition
    change, no new phase from the diagram, nothing but path.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> annealing, normalising and tempering are three words
    for the same thing — "making it softer". They are different itineraries with different
    targets. Annealing is a <em>reset</em>: full equilibrium, maximum softness, for forming
    operations. Normalising is a <em>cleanup</em>: air cooling to erase a prior
    treatment's coarse or non-uniform grain. Tempering is a <em>partial retreat</em>:
    it starts from martensite and gives back just enough hardness to buy toughness. Run
    the wrong one and the part fails its job — temper a normalized steel and nothing
    changes, because there is no martensite to relax.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> quenching makes steel hard because it "cools it fast" or
    "freezes it strong". Nothing about speed itself adds strength. The quench works by
    <em>preventing diffusion</em> — it is the mechanical suppression of Fick's law — so that
    a metastable structure (martensite) forms that equilibrium would never allow. Cool the
    same steel too slowly and you get soft pearlite no matter how dramatically the water
    boils.
  </p>

  <h2>Precipitation hardening: ageing on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    The aerospace heat treatment is a slower race, run deliberately. In the Al–Cu system
    (2024 aluminium), copper dissolves plentifully at ~540 °C but almost not at all at room
    temperature. The schedule: heat to dissolve (solution treat), quench to trap a
    <strong>supersaturated solid solution</strong>, then <strong>age</strong> at 150–190 °C —
    warm enough for copper to migrate, cool enough that equilibrium is still far away. The
    copper precipitates as nanoscale clusters (Guinier–Preston zones, then metastable θ″/θ′
    plates) spaced tens of nanometres apart — precisely the obstacle field dislocations
    cannot slice through cheaply. The numbers are dramatic: 2024 in the annealed O
    condition yields about 75 MPa; after solution treatment and natural ageing to T4 it
    yields around 325 MPa — more than fourfold — and artificially aged 7075-T6 exceeds
    500 MPa. The airframe's strength is a scheduled precipitation, which is why the spec
    sheet's T-temper is really an ageing recipe: T4 means solution-treated and naturally
    aged, T6 means solution-treated and artificially aged. The same physics strengthens
    nickel superalloys (γ′ precipitates) and the newest high-strength steels. The delicate
    part is <strong>overageing</strong>: hold too long or
    too hot and the precipitates coarsen (big ones eat small ones — Ostwald ripening),
    spacing widens, strength falls. Coarsening follows the Lifshitz–Slyozov–Wagner law:
    mean particle radius grows as t^(1/3) at fixed temperature, so doubling a precipitate's
    radius takes eight times the hold — slow enough to catch in process control, but the
    rate constant itself carries an exponential temperature dependence, which is why a few
    degrees too hot ages an alloy faster than a few hours too long ever could. Ageing is a
    thermostat-and-calendar operation with a peak, and quality control is the discipline
    of hitting the peak.
  </p>

  <h2>Case hardening: diffusion as a design tool</h2>
  <p>
    A gear tooth wants impossible properties at once — hard, wear-resistant surface;
    tough, shock-absorbing core. Diffusion resolves the contradiction by giving the part a
    composition <em>gradient</em>: <strong>carburising</strong> bakes low-carbon steel in a
    carbon-rich atmosphere at ~900–950 °C, so carbon seeps inward, then quench-and-temper
    hardens only the carbon-rich skin. Quantify with the rule above: carbon in austenite at
    950 °C has D ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻¹¹ m²/s (the same Arrhenius line as before), so an hour gives
    √(Dt) ≈ 0.2 mm of case; a ten-hour soak reaches roughly 0.6 mm — case depth is literally
    a time bill. Variants: <strong>nitriding</strong>
    (nitrogen at ~500 °C, below transformation temperature, minimal distortion — used where
    precision survives) and <strong>flame/induction hardening</strong> (no chemistry — heat
    only the surface past transformation and quench it before the core notices). Differential
    quenching is ancient: the Japanese smith's clay-coated blade — thick insulation on the
    spine, bare steel at the edge — produced hard edge, tough spine and the visible hamon
    line, centuries before TTT diagrams explained why it worked.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The phase transformations underneath.</strong> Pearlite, bainite and martensite
    are not phases but <em>transformation products</em> with distinct mechanisms: pearlite
    grows by coupled diffusion (ferrite and cementite lamellae advancing together — the
    eutectoid's cooperative front); bainite is a mid-temperature hybrid (shear plus
    carbon partitioning); martensite is pure shear — a military, diffusionless transformation
    where the lattice distorts in a coordinated wave, which is why it can outrun any cooling
    rate and why each martensite plate forms in microseconds. Transformation
    <strong>nucleation and growth</strong> theory (nucleation barrier ∝ interface energy
    over driving force; growth ∝ diffusion) unifies all of it, and explains the TTT curve's
    shape quantitatively. Homogeneous nucleation of a new phase inside a parent grain is so
    penalised by interface energy that real transformations lean on defects — grain
    boundaries, dislocations — as nucleation sites; the defect lesson's "flaws" turn out to
    be the transformation's doorways.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The violence budget.</strong> Quenches are thermal shock: surface contracts
    around a hot core, generating stresses that warp and crack parts. Half of heat-treatment
    engineering moderates the other half — oil, salt and polymer quenchants with tuned
    cooling curves (fast through the nose, slow through martensite start, which is exactly
    the wrong order for plain water), interrupted quenching (austempering, martempering),
    press quenching to hold geometry, and cryogenic treatment to finish transforming retained
    austenite. Additive manufacturing has made this budget central: 3-D-printed metals
    experience thousands of micro-weld thermal cycles, leaving residual stress and mixed
    microstructures that post-treatments (stress relief, hot isostatic pressing to close
    pores, full re-solutionising) must reconcile — process and heat treatment are now one
    design space, not two.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tempering, quantified.</strong> Tempering is a sequence of staged reactions,
    not one softening event: stage one (about 100–250 °C) precipitates the transition
    carbide ε (roughly Fe<sub>2.4</sub>C) out of supersaturated martensite; stage two
    (200–300 °C) decomposes any retained austenite; stage three (250–400 °C) replaces ε
    with cementite and lets the dislocation cell structure recover; stage four, at the
    highest temperatures, recrystallises the ferrite matrix. Because hardness depends on
    all of these together, engineers compress the whole schedule into the
    <strong>Hollomon–Jaffe parameter</strong>, HJP = T·(C + log t) with T in kelvin, t in
    hours and C ≈ 20 for plain-carbon steels: any time–temperature pairs with equal HJP
    deliver equal hardness. One hour at 300 °C scores 573 × 20 ≈ 11 500; matching that at
    250 °C requires 523 × (20 + log t) = 11 500, i.e. about 80 hours — the mathematical
    form of the shop-floor rule that each extra 25 °C of tempering temperature is worth
    many extra hours. Two ambushes live inside the tempering range:
    <strong>tempered-martensite embrittlement</strong> near 300–350 °C, where cementite
    films form on lath boundaries, and (reversible) <strong>temper embrittlement</strong>
    at 375–575 °C, where phosphorus, tin and antimony segregate to prior-austenite grain
    boundaries — cured by rapid cooling through the range or by molybdenum additions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where martensite starts.</strong> The martensite-start temperature M<sub>s</sub>
    falls almost linearly with alloy content; the classic Andrews regression,
    M<sub>s</sub> (°C) ≈ 539 − 423·(%C) − 30.4·(%Mn) − 17.7·(%Ni) − 12.1·(%Cr) − 7.5·(%Mo),
    is the workhorse. For the eutectoid 0.8 % C steel it predicts about 200 °C, so such a
    steel only finishes transforming as it nears room temperature. Below M<sub>s</sub> the
    transformed fraction rises with undercooling roughly as the Koistinen–Marburger
    relation, f = exp(−0.011·(M<sub>s</sub> − T)); austenite that survives to room
    temperature is <em>retained austenite</em> — soft, and liable to transform slowly in
    service, wrecking the dimensional stability of gauges and bearings. Precision parts
    therefore take a cryogenic trip to −80 °C or below (cold treatment with liquid
    nitrogen) followed by a further temper, closing the transformation on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Predicting martensite-start temperatures and quench
    distortion from first principles remains partly empirical — distortion simulation is
    good but not perfect, and "safcracking" (quench cracks in high-carbon grades) still
    surprises. Diffusion data at the low temperatures of battery electrodes and solder joints
    is thin but increasingly load-bearing. And the newest frontier is <em>processing
    without melting</em>: solid-state additive routes and severe plastic deformation create
    microstructures no furnace itinerary can, challenging the assumption that heat treatment
    is the only way to reset a metal's internal state. The race between thermodynamics and
    kinetics still has unexplored tracks.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Callister &amp; Rethwisch, <em>Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction</em>; Porter &amp; Easterling, <em>Phase Transformations in Metals and Alloys</em>; ASM Handbook Vol. 4, <em>Heat Treating</em>; Krauss, <em>Steels: Processing, Structure, and Performance</em>; the phase-diagram lesson of this ladder.</p>
